WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.240
A train is heading towards five people.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.580
You can save them,
but only by killing your own sibling.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.520
What would you do?

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.090
Imagine you're standing
beside a railway track.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.570
A runaway train is approaching
five people who cannot escape.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.830
There's a lever next to you.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.490
Pulling it will redirect the
train onto another track.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.540
But there's one person
on that second track.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.100
And this time, it's not a stranger.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.650
It's your own brother or sister.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.310
You have seconds to decide.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.370
If you pull the lever, you save five
lives but sacrifice someone you love.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.640
If you do nothing, five people die,

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.700
but you didn't actively redirect
the train towards your sibling.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.620
So which decision is morally right?

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.210
This is a variation of the
famous Trolley Problem,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.910
a philosophical thought experiment
about morality and difficult choices.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.580
Does saving the greatest number
always justify the sacrifice?

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.480
Forget what sounds morally correct.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.860
What would YOU actually do?

